schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: On-chain attestations cannot be changed, deleted, or faked once recorded
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    "Once it's there, it cannot be changed, deleted, or faked. It's like
    carving something into stone, it stays forever." (onchain-attestations.md,
    describing attestations once written to a blockchain)
  evidence: >-
    No citation given in the article body; `sources: []` in the article's own
    frontmatter. The claim reflects a generally accurate property of
    append-only, sufficiently-decentralized blockchains (data written to a
    sufficiently secure chain is very difficult to alter retroactively) but is
    stated here as an absolute with no caveats.
  source_lineage: "src/content/docs/onchain-attestations.md (frontmatter sources field is empty)"
  interpretation: >-
    Presented to a beginner, non-technical audience as an unqualified
    guarantee, to build confidence in attestations as a credentialing tool.
  uncertainty: >-
    Overstated as an absolute. In practice: (1) an attestation being
    "unchangeable" refers to the record of the claim, not the truth of the
    claim — a false or mistaken attestation is just as permanently recorded as
    a true one; (2) revocation schemes exist precisely because issuers
    sometimes need to invalidate an attestation; (3) "cannot be faked" depends
    entirely on trusting whoever the issuing party is, which the article does
    not address. The article's own `sources: []` frontmatter field confirms no
    supporting citation was attached.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/onchain-attestations.md
    transformation: quoted
    authorship: ai-assisted
